The program is small: read one line with [38;5;10mfgets[0m, parse it with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, then print four lines per [38;5;10mrun[0m call twice over the same mutated [38;5;10mHouse[0m. Faithfulness required emulating three C details rather than reaching for Rust idioms:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfgets[0m semantics — at most 99 bytes, stops after a kept newline, and the 100-byte buffer is pre-zeroed, so the effective string ends at the first NUL. Implemented with [38;5;10mtake(99).read_until(b'\n', ...)[0m plus truncation at a NUL. [38;5;10mStdin::read_line[0m would have been wrong (no length cap, rejects invalid UTF-8, no NUL handling).[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics — hand-rolled base-10 parser that skips C-locale whitespace, accepts an optional sign, accepts trailing garbage ([38;5;10m"5abc"[0m → 5), returns [38;5;10mendptr == str[0m on no conversion, and clamps with [38;5;10mERANGE[0m on [38;5;10mlong[0m overflow. [38;5;10mstr::parse[0m differs on all of these.[0m[0m
- The original's quirks are preserved, not fixed: the unchecked [38;5;10mfgets[0m return (empty stdin falls through to [38;5;10m"An error occurred"[0m), the missing full-string-consumed check, and [38;5;10mbedrooms += extra[0m wrapping ([38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m, so debug builds don't panic where C wraps).[0m[0m
